  "id": "DrugBank_DB00792 - MONDO_10481",
  "verbalization": "Tripelennamine is associated with Angioedema through a chain of indications that link it to Urticaria, a condition that is further linked to other drugs. The evidence suggests that Tripelennamine may share a mechanism of action with drugs like Bamipine, Rupatadine, and Pheniramine, all of which are indicated to cause Angioedema. These drugs are commonly used to treat Urticaria, and their association with Angioedema implies a potential overlap in pathophysiological mechanisms, such as mast cell activation or histamine release. The cumulative scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The presence of multiple drugs with similar indications for Urticaria and Angioedema suggests that Tripelennamine may be part of a broader class of compounds that affect mast cell function, potentially leading to angioedema. This integrated evidence points to a possible role for Tripelennamine in the development of Angioedema through its interaction with Urticaria, though further research is needed to confirm this hypothesis.",
